    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>To file a home insurance claim, make the property safe, prevent further damage, document everything, read your policy, and report the loss to your insurer as soon as possible. From there, you work with the adjuster, review the estimate, submit any proof of loss, and track the payment against Texas deadlines.</p>
<p>This guide walks Texas homeowners through how to file a home insurance claim, one step at a time, with a checklist at the end. It applies to fire, wind, hail, plumbing leaks, theft and most other homeowners losses.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Make Sure Everyone Is Safe</h2>
<p>Nothing in the claim matters more than people. After a fire, storm or major leak, stay out of the house until it is safe. Watch for downed power lines, gas smells, sagging ceilings and wet electrical outlets.</p>
<p>If you smell gas, leave and call the utility from outside. If water is near the breaker panel or outlets, do not touch it; call an electrician. After a fire, wait for the fire department to clear the structure before going in.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Prevent Further Damage With Reasonable Repairs</h2>
<p>Nearly every homeowners policy requires you to protect the property from further damage. The Texas Department of Insurance (TDI) recommends covering broken windows, tarping roof holes and removing standing water, while waiting on permanent repairs until your insurer approves them.</p>
<ul>
<li>Tarp the roof or board up openings.</li>
<li>Shut off the water main if a pipe burst.</li>
<li>Run fans and a dehumidifier to start drying wet areas.</li>
<li>Move undamaged belongings to a dry, secure place.</li>
<li>Save every receipt; your policy may reimburse reasonable temporary repairs.</li>
</ul>
<p>[[product:heavy-duty-tarp]]</p>
<p>Photograph damage before you start, then photograph the temporary fix. Keep damaged items, such as a burst supply line or broken shingles, until the insurer says you can discard them.</p>

<h2>Step 3: Document the Damage Thoroughly</h2>
<p>TDI suggests writing down the date and time you first saw the damage and the weather at the time, then photographing all of it. Good documentation is what separates a smooth claim from a long argument.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Photos and video:</strong> wide shots of each room and the exterior, then close-ups of every damaged area.</li>
<li><strong>Measurements:</strong> room sizes and the extent of damaged drywall, flooring and roofing.</li>
<li><strong>Contents list:</strong> damaged items with brand, age, approximate cost and a photo.</li>
<li><strong>Moisture readings:</strong> for water losses, readings in the same spots over several days.</li>
<li><strong>Weather records:</strong> for storm claims, the date of the hail, wind or rain event.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Step 4: Read Your Policy and Declarations Page</h2>
<p>Before you call, spend 15 minutes with your declarations page and policy. You want to know:</p>
<ul>
<li>Your deductible, and whether wind and hail have a separate, often percentage-based, deductible.</li>
<li>Whether you have replacement cost or actual cash value coverage on the dwelling, roof and contents.</li>
<li>Your loss of use limit, if you cannot stay in the home.</li>
<li>Key exclusions, such as flood, mold limits or gradual leaks.</li>
<li>Your "duties after loss," including notice requirements and any proof of loss deadline.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you do not have a copy, ask your agent or insurer for a complete copy of the policy with every endorsement. If you cannot live in the home, our guide to <a href="/blog/loss-of-use-coverage/">loss of use coverage</a> explains what your insurer will pay for temporary housing.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Notify Your Insurer and Get a Claim Number</h2>
<p>Report the loss promptly. TDI notes that you can call the company, file online or use its mobile app. Write down your claim number, the date, and the name of everyone you speak with.</p>
<p>Before filing, compare the likely repair cost with your deductible. If the damage is close to or under the deductible, you may receive little or nothing. That said, if you are unsure how extensive damage is, report it. Late notice is a common reason claims get disputed.</p>
<p>On the first call, stick to facts you know: what happened, when you found it, which areas are damaged and what temporary steps you have taken. Avoid guessing about causes or dollar amounts. Ask what documents the insurer needs, how to submit them, and when to expect an adjuster.</p>
<p>From here on, start a claim diary. It can be a notebook or a spreadsheet, but log every call, email, inspection, request and promise, with dates. A diary turns "they said they would pay that" into a dated record.</p>

<h2>Step 6: Meet the Adjuster and Review the Estimate</h2>
<p>The insurer will assign an adjuster, who may inspect in person or review photos. Be there for the inspection and walk the adjuster through every damaged area, including the attic, crawl spaces and closets. Hand over a copy of your photos and contents list.</p>
<p>After the inspection, the adjuster writes a scope of loss and an estimate. Read it line by line:</p>
<ul>
<li>Does the scope include every damaged room and item?</li>
<li>Are the quantities, such as square feet of drywall or squares of roofing, accurate?</li>
<li>Does it include necessary items such as removal, debris haul-off, paint and code-required work?</li>
<li>How much depreciation was subtracted, and is it recoverable?</li>
</ul>
<p>Get your own estimate from a licensed, reputable contractor and send the insurer any differences in writing. If you feel outmatched on a large loss, our comparison of <a href="/blog/public-adjuster-vs-insurance-adjuster/">public adjusters vs. insurance adjusters</a> explains who works for whom.</p>

<h2>Step 7: Submit a Proof of Loss if Requested</h2>
<p>Some insurers ask for a sworn proof of loss, a signed statement of the amount you are claiming and the facts of the loss. Your policy explains when one is required and how long you have to submit it. Read it carefully, attach supporting documents and keep a copy. Do not leave items off just because you have not finished counting; ask whether you can supplement later.</p>

<h2>Texas Prompt Payment Deadlines</h2>
<p>Chapter 542 of the Texas Insurance Code, the Prompt Payment of Claims Act, sets deadlines for most insurers. TDI summarizes them on its <a href="https://tdi.texas.gov/blog/insurance-claim-deadlines.html">insurance claim deadlines page</a>.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr><th>Step</th><th>Deadline under Chapter 542</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Acknowledge the claim, begin investigating and request needed items</td><td>Within 15 days after notice of the claim</td></tr>
<tr><td>Accept or reject in writing</td><td>Within 15 business days after receiving all requested items, statements and forms</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extension</td><td>Up to 45 more days, if the insurer explains why it needs more time</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pay an accepted claim</td><td>Within 5 business days after notifying you it will pay (or after you perform a required act)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Weather catastrophe or major natural disaster</td><td>Deadlines extended by 15 additional days</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Surplus lines insurers have longer acknowledgment and payment windows, and the Texas Windstorm Insurance Association follows its own rules. If an insurer rejects a claim, it must explain why in writing. For more on fighting a denial, see our guide to a <a href="/blog/water-damage-claim-denied/">denied water damage claim</a>.</p>

<h2>Step 8: Getting Paid, Lenders and Recoverable Depreciation</h2>
<p>If you have a mortgage, expect your lender's name on the check. According to TDI's guide to <a href="https://tdi.texas.gov/tips/getting-your-insurance-claim-paid.html">getting your insurance claim paid</a>, the mortgage company must contact you within 10 days of receiving the payment, and you then work with it to release funds for repairs. Lenders often release money in stages as work is inspected.</p>
<p>With replacement cost coverage, TDI explains that you may receive two payments: the first covers estimated repair costs minus depreciation, and the second comes after repairs are done. That second payment is often called recoverable depreciation. To collect it, you usually need to complete the repairs and send the insurer invoices or proof of completion within the time your policy allows.</p>
<p>[[tool:insurance-claim-depreciation-calculator]]</p>
<p>If you disagree with the amount, provide documentation supporting your number. When the dispute is only about the dollar amount, your policy's <a href="/blog/appraisal-clause-insurance/">appraisal clause</a> may offer a faster path than a lawsuit. For a large or disputed claim, confirm your options with a licensed public adjuster or Texas insurance attorney.</p>

<h2>Home Insurance Claim Checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li>Get everyone out safely and secure utilities.</li>
<li>Make temporary repairs and keep receipts.</li>
<li>Photograph and video all damage before cleanup.</li>
<li>Write down when you found the damage and the weather that day.</li>
<li>Read your declarations page, deductibles and exclusions.</li>
<li>Report the claim and record the claim number.</li>
<li>Start a claim diary of every call and email.</li>
<li>Attend the adjuster inspection and share your documentation.</li>
<li>Compare the insurer's estimate with your own contractor's estimate.</li>
<li>Submit a proof of loss if requested, before the deadline.</li>
<li>Track Chapter 542 deadlines against your diary.</li>
<li>Coordinate with your lender on endorsing and releasing the check.</li>
<li>Finish repairs and submit invoices to recover depreciation.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Bottom Line</h2>
<p>Filing a home insurance claim goes best when you act fast and keep records. Stop further damage, document it before cleanup, know your policy, report promptly and keep a claim diary. Review the estimate line by line, hold the insurer to Texas deadlines, and finish repairs on time so you collect the depreciation you are owed.</p>
